@extends('layouts.app')

@section('title')Connecting@endsection
@section('description')How to open an Orbit database with Database::connect, set the connection pool's limits with connect_with and PoolOptions, check it with ping, use the raw pool, and close it.@endsection

@section('content')
  <h1>{{ $title }}</h1>
  <p>A <code>Database</code> is the connection pool for one database. Connect once, at startup, and share the value with the routes, for example in Rocket's managed state or behind an <code>Arc</code>. A <code>Database</code> is not <code>Clone</code>.</p>

  <h2>Connect</h2>
  <p>Pass the connection URL to <code>Database::connect</code>. The URL's scheme chooses the database: <code>mysql://user:password@host/database</code> for MySQL, <code>postgres://user:password@host/database</code> for PostgreSQL, or <code>sqlite:///path/to/app.db?mode=rwc</code> for SQLite. The example uses MySQL:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">use orbit_orm::Database;

let db = Database::connect("mysql://user:password@127.0.0.1/app").await?;</code></pre>
  <p>A URL that the server rejects, such as a wrong password, returns an <code>Error</code>. See <a href="/docs/errors">Errors</a>.</p>

  <h2>Ping</h2>
  <p><code>ping</code> runs <code>SELECT 1</code> and returns an error when the database does not answer. It is a simple way to check the connection at startup:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">db.ping().await?;</code></pre>

  <h2>The pool</h2>
  <p><code>connect</code> uses the default pool settings: at most 5 connections open at once, a request that finds none free waits up to 10 seconds and then fails, and no connection is kept open while idle. <code>connect_with</code> takes a <code>PoolOptions</code> to change them:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">use std::time::Duration;
use orbit_orm::{Database, PoolOptions};

let options = PoolOptions {
    max_connections: 20,
    acquire_timeout: Duration::from_secs(5),
    ..PoolOptions::default()
};
let db = Database::connect_with("mysql://user:password@127.0.0.1/app", options).await?;</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><code>max_connections</code>, the most connections the pool opens at once. Further requests wait for one to be released.</li>
    <li><code>acquire_timeout</code>, how long a request waits for a connection before it fails.</li>
    <li><code>min_connections</code>, the connections the pool keeps open while idle. It is 0 by default.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The raw pool</h2>
  <p><code>db.pool()</code> gives the <code>sqlx</code> <code>MySqlPool</code>, for queries that Orbit does not wrap yet. It panics when the database is not MySQL, so check <code>driver()</code> first, or use <code>postgres_pool()</code> or <code>sqlite_pool()</code>, which return an <code>Option</code>. Inside a transaction, <code>tx.connection()</code> gives the transaction's MySQL connection, and panics on other databases. See <a href="/docs/transactions">Transactions</a>.</p>

  <h2>Close</h2>
  <p><code>close</code> closes every connection in the pool:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">db.close().await;</code></pre>
@endsection
